    "description": "Aqara's U100 sits at the more enthusiast end of the category. It brings a fingerprint reader and Apple Home Key support to a deadbolt that integrates naturally with Aqara's sensors, so unlocking the door can trigger a scene such as turning on lights and disarming a camera. The manufacturer lists compatibility with Apple Home and with other platforms through its own hub, which matters because the more advanced features lean on that hub being present. This is not the lock to pick if you want the simplest possible setup or the largest support network; Yale, Schlage and Kwikset have deeper retail and service reach. But for someone who already has an Aqara hub, a handful of sensors and a taste for automations, it is the most natural lock to add."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Smart Locks",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-smart-lock/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best smart lock overall?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most front doors, a keypad deadbolt from an established brand such as Yale or Schlage is the safest choice, because the keypad works for everyone and the lock keeps working when the app does not. Pick the connectivity version that matches how you will use it, and confirm the exact model number before you buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are smart locks safe from hackers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Reputable smart locks use encrypted communication, and real-world incidents tend to come from shared codes, weak passwords or unlocked phones rather than attacks on the lock. Use a unique password, turn on two-factor authentication, install firmware updates and delete unused codes. A physical key override is a sensible backup."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens to a smart lock when the power or internet goes out?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A keypad lock runs on its own batteries and checks codes internally, so it keeps working through power and internet outages. Remote features and cloud schedules pause until the connection returns. Keep a physical key or emergency battery contact available so an outage never leaves you stranded."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install a smart lock myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually yes. Manufacturers generally describe replacement deadbolts as a screwdriver job on a standard prepped door, and retrofit models are often simpler. Measure the backset, door thickness and bore hole first, and check the listing for compatibility. Mortise locks and unusual doors may need a locksmith or a conversion kit."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need Wi-Fi for a smart lock?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not necessarily. A Bluetooth keypad lock works with codes and with your phone when you are nearby. Wi-Fi, either built in or through a bridge accessory, adds remote lock and unlock and alerts when you are away. Wi-Fi also drains batteries faster, so buy it only if you will use remote access."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do smart lock batteries last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturers commonly quote months to a year or more on four AA batteries, with Bluetooth-only models lasting longer than Wi-Fi models. Cold weather, frequent use and weak signal shorten that. Plan to check the cells twice a year, and treat the first low-battery warning as a deadline rather than a suggestion."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can renters use a smart lock?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often yes, if you choose a retrofit model that mounts over the interior thumb-turn and leaves the exterior hardware alone, such as the August Wi-Fi Smart Lock. Check your lease first, since some landlords require approval. Retrofit locks are easy to remove, so you can take the lock with you when you move."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

