        <div id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Choose: Replacement Deadbolt, Retrofit or Latch
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first decision is physical, not digital. There are three shapes of smart lock. A replacement deadbolt takes out your existing lock and puts in a new one with a keypad on the outside; Yale, Schlage, Kwikset and Ultraloq make these. A retrofit lock leaves your exterior hardware and your keys alone and motorizes the lock from the inside or swaps the internal mechanism; August and Level are the examples here. A latch or lever set replaces a door handle and latch rather than a deadbolt, and suits interior doors or a door with no deadbolt. For a front door, almost everyone should pick between the first two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a replacement deadbolt if you want a keypad. A keypad is the single feature that most improves life with a smart lock, because it works for everyone in the house, including people who do not want an app, children who do not have a phone and guests who need temporary access. Choose a retrofit if you rent, if you love the look of your existing hardware, or if you need your existing keys to keep working. The trade-off is that a retrofit usually means the phone or a key is your way in, unless you add an accessory.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lock Shape</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch Out For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Replacement deadbolt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Front doors where you want a keypad</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Door must be prepped for a standard deadbolt</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Retrofit (inside mount)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Renters and period doors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Phone or key is your way in without an accessory</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hidden retrofit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keeping the original look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Must match your deadbolt brand and backset</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Thread, Z-Wave and Home Key</h3>

          <p>
            Connectivity is where most buyers get confused, so here is the short version. Bluetooth connects the lock to your phone when you are nearby and is the most battery-friendly option. Wi-Fi connects the lock to your router so you can control it from anywhere without a hub, at the cost of faster battery drain. Z-Wave and Zigbee are low-power mesh protocols that need a compatible hub, and suit people who already run a hub-based smart home. Thread is a newer low-power mesh network that Matter-compatible devices use, and it needs a Thread border router, which many recent smart speakers and displays provide. Apple Home Key is a feature that lets an iPhone or Apple Watch unlock a compatible lock by tapping it, using the same wallet technology as contactless payments.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the connectivity to the way you will actually use the lock. If you only want the keypad and occasional phone control when home, Bluetooth is plenty and the batteries last longest. If you want to check the door from work or let in a visitor remotely, you need Wi-Fi built in or a bridge. If you already have a hub, a Z-Wave or Thread version integrates more cleanly and uses less power. And if every phone in the house is an iPhone, Home Key is the feature that will make the lock feel effortless.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical caution applies to every brand: many models are sold as families, and the Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and Home Key versions look nearly identical on the shelf. Read the exact model number on the listing before you click buy, since the remote-access feature you expected may require a different SKU or an add-on bridge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="security-grades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Security: ANSI/BHMA Grades, Codes and Realistic Risks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Door lock quality in North America is rated by ANSI/BHMA standards, which grade residential locks on how well they resist forced entry and cycling. Grade 1 is the highest tier and is usually described as commercial-grade or best-in-class for residential use. Grade 2 is a solid mid-tier, and Grade 3 is the baseline that still meets the standard. When a manufacturer states a grade, treat it as a useful starting point and check the exact listing, because grades can differ between models in the same family.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the grade measures is mostly the mechanical lock: how much force the bolt and strike can take and how many cycles the mechanism survives. It does not measure the app, the radio or the code system. That is why it is sensible to evaluate a smart lock on two layers, the deadbolt and the electronics, and to buy the deadbolt grade you would have wanted anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the electronics side, the realistic risks are far more mundane than the headlines suggest. The most common failure is a human one: a code shared too widely, a phone left unlocked, or a password reused across accounts. Turn on two-factor authentication for the manufacturer's app, use a unique password, and delete codes you no longer need. Set an auto-lock so a door left ajar does not stay unlocked all afternoon, and keep a physical key somewhere you can reach it. Firmware updates matter too; the better manufacturers push security fixes through their apps, so install them rather than ignoring the notification.
          </p>

          <p>
            The strongest advice is to treat the lock as one layer. A reinforced strike plate with three-inch screws into the stud does more against a kick-in than any electronics, and it costs a few dollars. If you are tightening up the door while you are at it, a <a href="/best-stud-finder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">stud finder</a> helps you find solid framing for those screws, and a <a href="/best-cordless-drills/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">cordless drill</a> makes quick work of the pilot holes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Installation: Door Prep, Backset and Handing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most smart locks are a one-evening DIY job with a screwdriver, and manufacturers generally say the replacement models install in well under an hour on a standard door. The reason people struggle is not the lock, it is the door. Before you order, measure three things: the backset, the door thickness and the bore hole. Backset is the distance from the edge of the door to the center of the lock hole, and it is almost always 2-3/8 inches or 2-3/4 inches. Most smart locks include an adjustable latch that handles both, but confirm it on the listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Door thickness matters because the lock spans it. Standard residential doors are about 1-3/4 inches thick, and most locks are built for that, with optional kits for thicker or thinner doors. The bore hole is the large hole through the face of the door, normally 2-1/8 inches, with a smaller hole through the edge for the latch. A standard prepped door will already have both. If your door has an older or unusual lock, such as a mortise lock, check carefully, because most consumer smart locks will not fit it without a conversion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handing is the direction the door swings, and it matters for lever-style locks and for some retrofit mounts. Stand outside the door facing it: if the hinges are on your left, it is a left-hand door. Many smart deadbolts are not handed, but some interior parts and some levers are, so look for the setting or the instruction step during installation.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you install, work in a calm order. Remove the old lock and keep the screws. Fit the new latch and make sure it sits flush. Install the exterior assembly, run the cable through, then attach the interior mounting plate and the battery cover. Check that the bolt extends and retracts freely with the door open before you test it with the door closed, since a bolt that rubs against a misaligned strike will wear out the motor. If the bolt drags, the fix is almost always to adjust or relocate the strike plate rather than to fight the lock. A <a href="/best-laser-level/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">laser level</a> can help you check that the door and frame are square if you suspect the door has sagged over the years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-and-failure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Batteries, Power Failures and What Happens When Things Go Wrong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most smart locks run on four AA batteries, and manufacturers commonly quote battery life in months to a year or more depending on the model and how often the lock is used. Bluetooth-only models tend to sit at the longer end, and Wi-Fi models at the shorter end, because the Wi-Fi radio draws more power. Treat any quoted battery life as an estimate: cold weather, frequent use, a weak signal and rechargeable cells all shorten it. Use quality alkaline batteries unless the manufacturer says otherwise, since cheap cells can leak and rechargeables often drop voltage faster than the lock expects.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every reputable smart lock warns you before the batteries die. You will get a low-battery alert in the app, a beep or a light on the keypad, and often several weeks of runtime after the first warning. The most common way to get locked out is ignoring that alert, so put a reminder in your calendar the day you install the lock and change the cells twice a year regardless.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the batteries do die, you still have options, and this is where the design of the lock matters. Many models have an exterior contact that lets you touch a nine-volt battery to the terminals to power the keypad just long enough to enter your code. Many also keep a traditional keyway as a physical override. Retrofit locks such as August and Level keep your existing key working, so a dead battery is an annoyance rather than an emergency. Models with no key override and no emergency power contact are the ones to avoid unless you have a reliable backup entry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power outages and internet outages are a separate matter. A keypad lock keeps working with the router down, because the code check happens inside the lock. Remote features and cloud-based schedules will pause until the connection returns. If you rely on remote access for a rental, a short outage can strand a guest, so give them a keypad code and a phone number rather than counting on the app. The best habit is to keep one non-electronic way in, a key in a lockbox or with a neighbor, and to test it once a year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="smart-home-integration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Smart Home Integration: Alexa, Google, Apple and Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            A smart lock becomes more useful when it talks to the rest of your home, but integration is also where claims drift furthest from reality. Voice control is the simplest example. Most major locks work with Alexa, Google Assistant and Apple Home in some form, and the manufacturer's compatibility page is the place to check which features you get. Be careful with one point: many voice assistants will lock a door by voice but require a PIN to unlock it, which is a sensible safety choice rather than a flaw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Matter is a smart home standard designed to let devices from different brands work together, and some newer locks support it directly or through a hub. In practice it is still maturing, and lock support is among the more limited categories. If Matter matters to you, look for explicit support on the specific model you are buying rather than assuming the brand has it everywhere. The same goes for Thread and Z-Wave: both are good low-power options when you already have a hub or border router that supports them, and a poor reason to buy a lock if you do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The automations that tend to pay off are simple. Auto-lock after a set time, so the door is never accidentally left open. A notification when a specific code is used, so you know the kids are home. A scene that turns on the hallway light when the door unlocks after dark. A good rule is to start with one or two automations and live with them for a month before adding more, since a lock that does too many clever things is a lock that surprises you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the ecosystem you are choosing, not just the lock. A lock that depends on one manufacturer's cloud is only as durable as that company's commitment to the product. Locks that support open standards, or that keep working locally with the keypad when the cloud is down, age better. If you are building out a connected front door, our <a href="/best-video-doorbell/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">video doorbell guide</a> and <a href="/best-garage-door-opener/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">garage door opener guide</a> cover the neighboring pieces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-and-deals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Smart Locks Cost and When to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Smart lock prices span a wide range, from budget Bluetooth models to premium Wi-Fi and Home Key units, and the spread usually follows connectivity. A keypad-only Bluetooth lock sits at the low end, a Wi-Fi keypad lock in the middle, and a Home Key or multi-protocol lock at the top. Because prices move constantly and vary by retailer, we are not going to print a number that will be wrong by the time you read it. Check the live price on the listing, and compare the same exact model number across sellers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The timing of deals follows a predictable pattern. Smart home devices are among the most heavily discounted categories during the big sales events, notably the Amazon Prime Big Deal Days in the fall, Black Friday and Cyber Monday in late November, and Prime Day in the summer. Manufacturers also run their own promotions, and older generations drop in price when a successor is announced. If you are not in a hurry, waiting for one of these windows is the single best way to save, and if you are in a hurry, it is still worth checking whether a coupon box is available on the listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Count the full cost, not just the sticker. A Wi-Fi bridge accessory, a spare set of batteries, a reinforced strike plate and a door prep kit add up. If you rent, factor in whether you can legally modify the lock, which is why retrofit models are so popular there. And consider whether any subscription is attached: most core lock functions are free, but some manufacturers charge for extended history or cloud features, so read the fine print before you commit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A note on our links: the product links on this page are affiliate links, which means we earn a commission if you buy through them, at no extra cost to you. It does not change which locks we recommend or the order we put them in. If a lock is not right for your door, we say so.
          </p>
        </div>

