        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Wrong Version of the Same Lock</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many smart locks come as a family of near-identical models. The Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and Home Key versions can look the same on the shelf but behave very differently. Read the exact model number and confirm that the connectivity you want is included, not an add-on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Door Measurements</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Backset, door thickness and bore hole decide whether a lock fits. Measure before you order, and check the listing for compatibility with your door. A lock that almost fits is a lock that jams, and a jammed bolt wears out the motor and drains the batteries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Low-Battery Warning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most common smart lock emergency is a door that will not open because the batteries died after weeks of warnings. Put a calendar reminder in on installation day, replace the cells twice a year, and keep a nine-volt battery or a spare key where you can reach it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the Lock as the Whole Security Plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A smart lock does not make a weak door strong. A short strike plate screwed into thin framing will fail long before the electronics do. Reinforce the strike with long screws into the stud and treat the lock as one layer alongside cameras and good habits.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Handing Out Permanent Codes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A shared code that never expires is a key you cannot get back. Give each person their own code, use scheduled access for contractors and guests, and delete codes when the arrangement ends. The activity log is only useful if each person has a separate credential.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

