        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best smart lock overall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most front doors, a keypad deadbolt from an established brand such as Yale or Schlage is the safest choice, because the keypad works for everyone and the lock keeps working when the app does not. Pick the connectivity version that matches how you will use it, and confirm the exact model number before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are smart locks safe from hackers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Reputable smart locks use encrypted communication, and real-world incidents tend to come from shared codes, weak passwords or unlocked phones rather than attacks on the lock. Use a unique password, turn on two-factor authentication, install firmware updates and delete unused codes. A physical key override is a sensible backup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens to a smart lock when the power or internet goes out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A keypad lock runs on its own batteries and checks codes internally, so it keeps working through power and internet outages. Remote features and cloud schedules pause until the connection returns. Keep a physical key or emergency battery contact available so an outage never leaves you stranded.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a smart lock myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes. Manufacturers generally describe replacement deadbolts as a screwdriver job on a standard prepped door, and retrofit models are often simpler. Measure the backset, door thickness and bore hole first, and check the listing for compatibility. Mortise locks and unusual doors may need a locksmith or a conversion kit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need Wi-Fi for a smart lock?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily. A Bluetooth keypad lock works with codes and with your phone when you are nearby. Wi-Fi, either built in or through a bridge accessory, adds remote lock and unlock and alerts when you are away. Wi-Fi also drains batteries faster, so buy it only if you will use remote access.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do smart lock batteries last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers commonly quote months to a year or more on four AA batteries, with Bluetooth-only models lasting longer than Wi-Fi models. Cold weather, frequent use and weak signal shorten that. Plan to check the cells twice a year, and treat the first low-battery warning as a deadline rather than a suggestion.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can renters use a smart lock?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often yes, if you choose a retrofit model that mounts over the interior thumb-turn and leaves the exterior hardware alone, such as the August Wi-Fi Smart Lock. Check your lease first, since some landlords require approval. Retrofit locks are easy to remove, so you can take the lock with you when you move.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

